[
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/Timeroot/IsoGraph\">IsoGraph</a> is a new graph theory library! The main stimulating objective was to be able to state things like <code>(cycle 5)ᶜ = cycle 5</code>, which requires a notion of \"graph as an object up to isomorphism\", i.e. the quotient type. This establishes that and builds up a lot of API around it. Why not build on Mathlib? It is also quite computationally oriented: it's finite graphs only, and every graph carries a <code>FinEnum</code> with it for performance purposes. Highlights:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A small nauty-like automorphism checker: individualisation–refinement with orbit pruning and backjumping, proved correct, which canonicalises a random 50-vertex graph in half a millisecond. Lets you reasonably test and prove equality. The algorithm is all verified in Lean as well, so it produces actual proofs. (Also lets you efficiently prove e.g. vertex transitivity or not, edge transitivity, etc.)</li>\n<li>A reasonably efficient graph enumerator, also with proved correctness, which can be used to check certain claims on small graphs. There's a <code>small_graphs</code> tactic to prove goals about graphs of bounded size.</li>\n<li>A <code>generate_graph_iso</code> tactic that repeatedly tries to decompose a graph into a description of simpler constructions. This is turn used to power lots of other automated computations. For instance, if I give you a graph as a random adjacency matrix, but it turns out that it can be decomposed through sums and complements into cycles/paths/complete graphs/kneser graphs, then this tactic will find that decomposition for you.</li>\n<li>This can in turn be used to automatically compute and prove values like independence number, chromatic number, and so on.</li>\n<li>A <code>graph_sat</code> tactic that converts goals about invariants into bv_decide'able form to get kernel-checked proofs of e.g. bounds on independence number or chromatic number.</li>\n<li>Tactics for exact calculation of fractional chromatic number (and some other LP-able invariants), borrowing the simplex algorithm to get an efficient certificate. This can also be used as a fast path in <code>graph_sat</code> if the fractional value + integrality provides an immediate proof.</li>\n<li>Essentially every result is stated initially for a <code>CGraph</code> type (a finite simple graph w/ bundled vertex type, FinEnum and decidable adjacency). The <code>@[toIsoGraph]</code> attribute automatically lifts these to a proof about <code>IsoGraph</code> (the quotient type of CGraph up to isomorphism). For <a href=\"https://github.com/Timeroot/IsoGraph/blob/main/IsoGraph/SmallGraphs/Equalities.lean#L778-L782\">example</a>,</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">toIsoGraph</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">disjUnion_cartesianProduct</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">disjUnionCartesianProduct</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CGraph</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊕</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">□</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃</span><span class=\"n\">cg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">□</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊕</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">□</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>gives a computable isomorphism showing how the cartesian graph product distributes over disjoint union; it automatically produces a corresponding</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsoGraph</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">disjUnion_cartesianProduct</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsoGraph</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊕</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">□</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">□</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊕</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">□</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A pretty long list of graph constructions, operations, and invariants. Including a few different (scoped) Semiring instances on IsoGraph for different sum/product operations, and even the unique factorization property on connected graphs! (Sabidussi-Vizing).</li>\n<li>Treatment of several kinds of substructures -- (induced) subgraphs, (induced) minors, subdivisions, etc. Relationships between them. And algorithms to check the substructure containment. (These are often NP hard, but the algorithms are also much better than brute force, and can do e.g. K5 minor testing efficiently.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I believe this is a form useful for a lot of computational graph theory. I recognize that it is generally _not_ very compatible with Mathlib's design, because a good chunk of facts are native_decide'd. Most of these are easily avoidable, but given that \"efficient computation\" and \"nice algorithms for isomorphism/enumeration\" was one of the first goals, I didn't want to sacrifice that. The automorphism generation has several proofs involving well-founded recursion and so on that are not easily amenable to kernel reduction, which is also a point of tension. I'm sure there are many things I've missed, though, so maybe others could help make this more Mathlib-friendly, I hope! :) Also, I'd just like to comment that Aristotle helped a lot with the development. <span aria-label=\"charm\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f9ff\" role=\"img\" title=\"charm\">:charm:</span></p>",
